==========================================================================
2D Navier-Stokes (vorticity-streamfunction) -- convergence study
==========================================================================
domain [0, 2pi)^2  |  nu = 0.1  |  Fourier pseudo-spectral + explicit RK4
exact solution: Taylor-Green vortex (smooth for all t)  |  T = 1.0

SCOPE: this measures the order of accuracy of a discretisation on a smooth
known solution. It says NOTHING about the Navier-Stokes existence-and-
smoothness problem, which is open (a 3D question). Every number below
comes from an actual run; nothing is extrapolated beyond the grids run.

Sanity checks (N = 64):
  max|psi_rec - psi_exact|      = 7.772e-16   (Poisson sign/scale)
  max|u_rec   - u_exact|        = 6.661e-16
  max|v_rec   - v_exact|        = 6.661e-16
  max|(u.grad)w| (Taylor-Green) = 1.127e-14
  -> the Taylor-Green advection term is IDENTICALLY ZERO (value above is
     roundoff), and its vorticity is a single exactly-representable mode.
     It is therefore a roundoff-level CORRECTNESS check, not a spatial-
     order measurement. See the WARNING in part (1) and part (2).

==========================================================================
(1) SPATIAL convergence -- Taylor-Green vortex
==========================================================================
T = 1.0, dt = 0.0005   (stability margin nu*K2max*dt = 1.64 at N=256; limit 2.785)

         N          dt        L2 error     order p
    --------------------------------------------
        32     5.0e-04      6.3264e-16            
        64     5.0e-04      7.0672e-16       -0.16
       128     5.0e-04      7.5514e-16       -0.10
       256     5.0e-04      6.6628e-16        0.18

WARNING: the Taylor-Green L2 error is at the ROUNDOFF FLOOR (~6.7e-16)
         at EVERY N, because the advection term is identically zero and the
         solution is one exactly-representable Fourier mode. The 'order'
         column above is roundoff noise, NOT the scheme's spatial order.
         The solver is correct (error ~ machine epsilon); the TEST CASE is
         degenerate for spatial convergence. Part (2) measures the real order.

==========================================================================
(2) SPATIAL convergence -- manufactured solution (full spectrum + genuine
    nonlinearity):  w_ex = e^(-2 nu t) P_r(x) P_r(y),  r = 0.7
==========================================================================
T = 1.0, dt = 0.0005

         N          dt        L2 error     order p
    --------------------------------------------
        16     5.0e-04      8.0960e-01            
        32     5.0e-04      1.0323e-01        2.97
        64     5.0e-04      1.3306e-03        6.28
       128     5.0e-04      4.6609e-07       11.48
       256     5.0e-04      5.8372e-14       22.93

    Exponential (spectral) convergence: the error falls by a large factor per
    doubling and the observed order GROWS with N -- the signature of a
    spectral method on an analytic solution, not a fixed algebraic order.

==========================================================================
(3) TEMPORAL convergence -- Taylor-Green, N = 64
==========================================================================
    The spatial part is exact for this case, so the ONLY error is time
    integration. Explicit-RK4 stability: dt < 0.01360 (nu*K2max = 204.8);
    nsteps = 20, 40 give dt = 0.05, 0.025 > that bound (unstable) and are
    therefore not used -- the sweep starts at nsteps = 80 to satisfy it.

     nsteps            dt        L2 error     order p
    ------------------------------------------------
         80     1.250e-02      5.3633e-14            
        160     6.250e-03      3.8868e-15        3.79
        320     3.125e-03      3.1959e-16        3.60
        640     1.563e-03      4.9523e-16       -0.63

    RK4 is 4th order, so p -> 4 while truncation dominates; the finest
    refinement reaches the roundoff floor (~1e-16) and its measured order
    degrades there (machine noise, not a solver defect).

==========================================================================
(4) ENSTROPHY decay -- Taylor-Green, N = 64, dt = 0.0005
==========================================================================
    Z(t) = <w^2>;  exact Z(t) = Z(0) e^(-4 nu t),  with Z(0) = 1.000000
    max relative drift over 0 <= t <= 1.0 :  3.074e-15

==========================================================================
(5) Plot
==========================================================================
  plot saved to D:\gh-opt\math-numerics-lab\results\ns2d_convergence.png

==========================================================================
ASSERTIONS
==========================================================================
    PASS: finest Taylor-Green L2 error 6.66e-16 < 1e-5
    PASS: manufactured-solution order at finest pair 22.93 >= 3 (spectral convergence)
    PASS: measured temporal order 3.79 >= 3.5 (RK4)
    PASS: max enstrophy drift 3.07e-15 < 1e-6

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
SUMMARY
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
    * Solver: Fourier pseudo-spectral, 2/3-rule dealiasing, explicit RK4.
    * Taylor-Green (the requested case) is reproduced to ROUNDOFF (~7e-16);
      it is degenerate for spatial convergence (zero advection, single mode)
      and is reported as a correctness check, not as a spatial order.
    * A non-degenerate manufactured solution shows SPECTRAL spatial
      convergence: error 8.1e-01 (N=16) -> 5.8e-14 (N=256),
      with the observed order growing to 22.9.
    * Temporal order on the exactly-representable Taylor-Green case is 3.79
      (RK4 ~ 4), measured while truncation dominates.
    * Discrete enstrophy tracks Z(0) e^(-4 nu t) to 3.1e-15 relative.

    * These are finite numerical measurements on smooth exact solutions.
      This says NOTHING about the Navier-Stokes existence-and-smoothness
      problem, which is open. No theorem is proved or claimed here.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
    All assertions passed: True
    Total wall time: 235.5 s
